The U.S. printed greenbacks in eighteen sixty-two to fight itself.

195
00:20:51,480 --> 00:20:56,560
Every belligerent in nineteen fourteen abandoned gold within weeks of the first shots.

196
00:20:57,460 --> 00:21:05,580
You cannot fund a large war with honest money, because honest money requires asking citizens to pay, and citizens, when

197
00:21:05,580 --> 00:21:12,720
asked, generally prefer to spend their limited savings on things other than killing strangers in countries they cannot find

198
00:21:12,720 --> 00:21:18,920
on a map. The Spanish-American War (eighteen ninety-eight) is what happens when you try.

199
00:21:19,740 --> 00:21:27,320
Fought entirely under the gold standard. The government had to fund it from existing revenue and a bond issue that required

200
00:21:27,320 --> 00:21:33,100
citizens to voluntarily hand over their gold. Duration: ten weeks.

201
00:21:33,860 --> 00:21:41,140
U.S. combat deaths: three hundred and forty-five. It ran out of enthusiasm approximately when it ran out of money.

202
00:21:41,920 --> 00:21:45,980
The gold standard did not prevent war. It prevented *cheap* war.

203
00:21:46,480 --> 00:21:49,940
It prevented war that outlasts the public's willingness to pay for it.

204
00:21:51,000 --> 00:21:56,360
On Wishonia, we display this information on a large sign outside our central resource building.

205
00:21:57,300 --> 00:22:02,920
The sign says: "LAST TIME WE LET SOMEONE PRINT MONEY FOR WAR: [DATE].

206
00:22:02,920 --> 00:22:07,620
DEATHS: [NUMBER]." It has not needed updating in four thousand years.

207
00:22:08,360 --> 00:22:17,300
Mostly because the sign is very effective. nineteen seventy-one: The Day Your Money Stopped Meaning Anything.

208
00:22:18,580 --> 00:22:27,840
August fifteen, nineteen seventy-one. A human named Nixon went on television and said, in slightly more presidential language:

209
00:22:28,540 --> 00:22:32,420
"Remember how dollars could be exchanged for gold? We're stopping that."

210
00:22:33,260 --> 00:22:42,020
"Also, wage and price controls." "Also, import taxes." He announced this on a Sunday evening in August, when nobody was

211
00:22:42,020 --> 00:22:49,300
watching, which is the human equivalent of robbing a house while the family is on vacation, except the family is two hundred

212
00:22:49,300 --> 00:22:55,420
million people and the house is their money. He called it a "New Economic Policy."

213
00:22:56,300 --> 00:23:03,040
On your planet, when someone names a policy the opposite of what it does, something terrible is about to happen.

214
00:23:03,680 --> 00:23:10,260
(See also: "Patriot Act," "Affordable Care Act," "Department of Defense," "Federal Reserve."

215
00:23:10,920 --> 00:23:16,140
It is not federal. It has no reserves. Two words, two lies.

216
00:23:16,220 --> 00:23:21,720
This is efficient, even by your standards.). Why He Did It.

217
00:23:21,780 --> 00:23:30,100
During World War Two, your government sold war bonds. Eighty-five million Americans bought one hundred and eighty-five billion

218
00:23:30,100 --> 00:23:35,420
dollars worth, because "buy bonds, beat the Nazis" is an easy poster.

219
00:23:36,380 --> 00:23:44,660
Vietnam was not an easy poster. You cannot run a bond drive for "help us spray carcinogenic chemicals on rice farmers and

220
00:23:44,660 --> 00:23:52,880
their children." So L.B.J. hid the war's cost inside the regular federal budget and covered the gap by printing money.

221
00:23:53,480 --> 00:24:01,940
No bond drives. No patriotic posters. Just quiet inflation, picking every pocket at two percent per year, which is below

222
00:24:01,940 --> 00:24:07,980
the human boredom threshold for investigating things. Foreign governments have lower boredom thresholds.

223
00:24:08,960 --> 00:24:16,640
France noticed first. Between nineteen sixty-three and nineteen sixty-six, de Gaulle secretly organized one hundred and

224
00:24:16,640 --> 00:24:24,260
twenty-nine flights and forty-four boat trips to extract three thousand three hundred and thirteen tonnes of French gold

225
00:24:24,260 --> 00:24:28,800
from American and British vaults before the Americans could finish spending it.

226
00:24:29,600 --> 00:24:39,240
He named the operation Vide-Gousset: "Empty Pocket". This is a heist film, except the person being robbed was also the person

227
00:24:39,240 --> 00:24:47,280
who started stealing first. By nineteen seventy-one, the gold was mostly gone and more countries were asking for theirs.

228
00:24:47,840 --> 00:24:51,440
Hence the speech. (Everyone else noticed fifty years later.).

229
00:24:52,720 --> 00:24:58,520
What He Killed. Before that speech, your species was running an experiment.

230
00:24:59,220 --> 00:25:03,060
You had anchored the dollar to gold at thirty-five dollars per ounce.

231
00:25:03,700 --> 00:25:10,100
You called it "Bretton Woods," after the hotel where you signed the agreement in nineteen forty-four, because your species

232
00:25:10,100 --> 00:25:14,180
names its most important decisions after the building they happened in.

233
00:25:14,760 --> 00:25:19,400
(See also: "The White House," "The Pentagon," "Lehman Brothers."

234
00:25:20,100 --> 00:25:24,940
The last one was a building full of brothers named Lehman who lost everyone's money.

235
00:25:25,600 --> 00:25:31,420
They named the crisis after the brothers, not the money. This is how your species assigns blame.).

236
00:25:32,780 --> 00:25:40,740
Here is how it worked, physically. Any foreign government could walk up to a window at the Federal Reserve, hand over thirty-five

237
00:25:40,740 --> 00:25:46,560
dollars, and receive one ounce of gold. Real gold. The kind you carry out in a box.

238
00:25:47,740 --> 00:25:54,660
This meant that if the U.S. printed too many dollars, governments would notice (because prices would drift above thirty-five

239
00:25:54,660 --> 00:26:01,420
dollars per ounce on the open market), and they would start showing up at the window, trading paper for metal, and walking

240
00:26:01,420 --> 00:26:05,820
out with your gold. The more you printed, the faster your vault emptied.

241
00:26:06,540 --> 00:26:12,640
This was not a rule. Rules can be rewritten by the same people who wrote them, usually at two a.m.

242
00:26:12,720 --> 00:26:16,860
when nobody is paying attention. This was a physical constraint.

243
00:26:17,180 --> 00:26:21,420
Gold is heavy. It leaves the building. You can see the shelves getting bare.

244
00:26:22,080 --> 00:26:27,000
The punishment for money-printing was not a sternly worded letter from an oversight committee.

245
00:26:27,500 --> 00:26:32,300
It was a foreign government loading your gold onto a cargo plane and flying it home.

246
00:26:33,100 --> 00:26:41,700
It worked. I know. I was surprised too. From nineteen forty-eight to nineteen seventy-three, the typical American family's

247
00:26:41,720 --> 00:26:46,660
income grew three percent per year, doubling roughly once a generation.

248
00:26:47,540 --> 00:26:54,880
Real G.D.P. grew four point two percent per year in the nineteen fifties and four point five percent in the nineteen sixties.

249
00:26:55,500 --> 00:27:01,200
Homeownership surged from forty-four percent to over sixty-two percent in a single generation.

250
00:27:02,060 --> 00:27:07,260
A single income could support a family of four, a mortgage, and a retirement.

251
00:27:07,980 --> 00:27:15,320
Income inequality fell to its lowest level of the century. The Gini coefficient trended down for two straight decades.

252
00:27:16,260 --> 00:27:24,100
This was not a coincidence. When money is anchored to something politicians can't print, politicians can't silently drain

253
00:27:24,100 --> 00:27:31,320
it. So the gains from productivity growth went to the people doing the work, not to whoever stands closest to the printer.

254
00:27:32,080 --> 00:27:36,120
Your economists will tell you this can't work. That a gold standard is a relic.

255
00:27:36,640 --> 00:27:43,140
That modern economies need "flexible monetary policy," which is a phrase meaning "we need to be able to print money without

256
00:27:43,140 --> 00:27:51,300
asking you.". Here is what they'd prefer you not check: every period in which your money was anchored to gold produced broad-based

257
00:27:51,300 --> 00:27:58,500
prosperity. Under the classical gold standard (eighteen seventy to nineteen fourteen), real G.D.P.

258
00:27:58,540 --> 00:28:02,080
per capita grew three point eight percent per year in the eighteen eighties.

259
00:28:02,860 --> 00:28:08,640
Between eighteen eighty and eighteen ninety-six, prices fell thirty percent and real G.D.P.

260
00:28:08,640 --> 00:28:16,480
increased eighty-five percent. Real wages rose. You built the railroad, the telephone, and the electric light, all while

261
00:28:16,480 --> 00:28:20,940
prices were falling. (The evidence on that particular period is detailed below.).

262
00:28:21,740 --> 00:28:26,100
Under Bretton Woods, you did it again. Twenty-seven years of anchored money.

263
00:28:26,780 --> 00:28:34,540
Twenty-seven years of the median family getting richer. Then Nixon unanchored it, and median income growth fell from nearly

264
00:28:34,540 --> 00:28:39,560
three percent to zero point six percent per year. An eighty percent collapse.

265
00:28:40,580 --> 00:28:48,340
Two experiments. Two gold-anchored systems. Both produced roughly double the median income growth of the fiat periods that

266
00:28:48,340 --> 00:28:53,120
followed them. Then you abandoned them both and the gains stopped reaching the median family.

267
00:28:53,860 --> 00:28:58,320
On Wishonia, when an experiment produces the same result twice, we call it "evidence."

268
00:28:59,300 --> 00:29:04,940
On Earth, you call it "outdated thinking" and go back to letting the people in the building print money for their friends.

269
00:29:05,900 --> 00:29:13,840
What Actually Happened. The gold standard died. Your species abandoned one shared hallucination (gold, which is a shiny

270
00:29:13,840 --> 00:29:20,460
rock you can't eat) for another shared hallucination (paper, which is a flat rock you also can't eat).

271
00:29:20,640 --> 00:29:27,640
You called this "modernization.". The dollar was now backed by "the full faith and credit of the United States government,"

272
00:29:28,100 --> 00:29:33,800
which is a phrase that sounds reassuring until you remember that the government was doing this specifically because it had

273
00:29:33,800 --> 00:29:40,260
run out of faith and credit. It's like a restaurant that can't pay its food suppliers announcing that meals are now backed

274
00:29:40,320 --> 00:29:44,220
by the full faith and credit of the restaurant. You'd leave.

275
00:29:44,340 --> 00:29:48,520
Except you can't leave the dollar because your rent is denominated in it.

276
00:29:48,660 --> 00:29:54,200
This is not a coincidence. The Dual Mandate (Or: The Two Goals of Serfdom).

277
00:29:54,940 --> 00:30:01,230
Your Federal Reserve has two official goals, like a mission statement, except the mission is you.

278
00:30:01,480 --> 00:30:07,700
"Maximum employment". "Stable prices" (which they define as two percent inflation per year).

279
00:30:08,560 --> 00:30:14,440
I read these several times to make sure I understood them. Then I read them again to make sure I hadn't misunderstood them.

280
00:30:15,120 --> 00:30:18,800
I had not. These are the actual goals. They are written down.

281
00:30:19,160 --> 00:30:26,040
On a website. You can look at them right now. Nobody is hiding them, which is the most disturbing part, because it means

282
00:30:26,040 --> 00:30:41,320
nobody thinks there's anything to hide. On Wishonia, our decentralized resource allocation system has one goal: "are beings'

283
00:30:41,320 --> 00:30:41,320
lives measurably improving?" But that's apparently too simple for a species that named its planet "dirt," so let me explain

284
00:30:41,320 --> 00:31:09,480
what your two goals actually do. Goal one: "Maximum Employment" (Keep Everyone Busy).

285
00:31:09,480 --> 00:31:12,280
"Maximum employment" means everyone has a job. It does not specify a good job.

286
00:31:12,360 --> 00:31:23,040
It does not specify a job that pays enough to eat. It does not specify a job that leaves you time to read about monetary

287
00:31:23,040 --> 00:31:37,820
policy. It specifies: occupied. A medieval serf working sunrise to sunset was maximally employed.

288
00:31:40,000 --> 00:31:41,420
A modern American working two jobs who can't afford a doctor's visit is also maximally employed.

289
00:31:41,420 --> 00:31:42,660
The metric cannot distinguish between these situations. From the metric's perspective, both serfs are performing optimally.

290
00:31:42,660 --> 00:31:49,840
I found this confusing at first. Then I realized it's not a bug.

291
00:31:50,160 --> 00:31:58,260
Busy people don't read Federal Reserve balance sheets. Busy people vote for whichever candidate promises to "create jobs,"

292
00:31:58,400 --> 00:32:01,480
which is a promise to keep them too busy to ask who's keeping them poor.

293
00:32:01,480 --> 00:32:09,360
A hamster wheel achieves maximum employment. Goal two: "Stable Prices" (Take two percent Per Year).

294
00:32:09,360 --> 00:32:46,120
"Stable prices" means two percent inflation per year. On Wishonia, "stable" means "not moving."

295
00:32:46,820 --> 00:32:51,480
On Earth, "stable prices" means prices that rise every year, predictably, forever.

296
00:32:51,480 --> 00:32:51,480
This is like calling a sinking ship "stable" because it sinks at a known rate.

297
00:33:44,110 --> 00:33:50,340
I need to say that again because your brain is going to try to skip past it.

298
00:33:51,120 --> 00:34:00,220
The Federal Reserve's official, publicly stated, announced-on-television goal is to make your money worth two percent less

299
00:34:00,220 --> 00:34:07,520
every year. They chose this number. They aim for it. They celebrate when they hit it.

300
00:34:08,239 --> 00:34:13,920
Over thirty-five years, two percent compounds to a fifty percent loss.

301
00:34:14,440 --> 00:34:20,080
Over the fifty-four years since Nixon, it has taken ninety-three percent.

302
00:34:20,739 --> 00:34:25,840
They named the target. They hit the target. You are the target.

303
00:34:26,719 --> 00:34:33,600
I am told these are three different sentences. The "Deflation Is Dangerous" Story.

304
00:34:34,460 --> 00:34:44,159
The justification for permanent inflation, taught in every economics class that bothers to exist: deflation (falling prices)

305
00:34:44,280 --> 00:34:50,480
is dangerous because "people won't buy things if they know prices will be lower tomorrow.".

306
00:34:51,199 --> 00:34:57,720
I watched your species for eighty years and I have some questions about this theory.

307
00:34:57,740 --> 00:35:04,520
You buy a new iPhone every two years knowing the next one will be better and cheaper.

308
00:35:05,080 --> 00:35:10,120
You bought a flat-screen TV knowing the price drops every year.

309
00:35:10,840 --> 00:35:16,340
You buy a laptop knowing it will be worth half as much in eighteen months.

310
00:35:16,880 --> 00:35:22,240
You bought a phone case for forty dollars that you will lose in three months.

311
00:35:22,760 --> 00:35:27,960
The entire technology sector has experienced continuous deflation for decades.

312
00:35:28,800 --> 00:35:34,240
It is the fastest-growing, most innovative, most profitable sector of your economy.

313
00:35:34,920 --> 00:35:41,560
People buy things they want. They do not sit in empty rooms waiting for prices to fall further.

314
00:35:42,400 --> 00:35:51,120
This is obvious to anyone who has met a human. Your species once stood in line for fourteen hours to buy a telephone.

315
00:35:51,660 --> 00:35:55,600
In the rain. Knowing a better one would exist in eleven months.

316
00:35:56,580 --> 00:36:04,560
The idea that you would stop buying things if they got cheaper is the single least credible claim ever made about a species

317
00:36:04,580 --> 00:36:12,380
that invented the shopping mall, the impulse buy, and Black Friday, which is a holiday where you celebrate gratitude by

318
00:36:12,380 --> 00:36:21,400
trampling each other for televisions. The One Data Point. The only evidence they cite is the Great Depression, where falling

319
00:36:21,400 --> 00:36:30,820
prices coincided with economic collapse. This is a correlation/causation error so basic it would fail an introductory statistics

320
00:36:30,820 --> 00:36:35,760
course at a university funded by the Federal Reserve. (Which is most of them.).

321
00:36:36,380 --> 00:36:42,840
The Great Depression's deflation was *caused by* bank failures and credit contraction, not the other way around.

322
00:36:43,560 --> 00:36:47,920
Blaming deflation for the Depression is like blaming wet streets for rain.

323
00:36:49,140 --> 00:36:57,300
Meanwhile, the longest sustained deflation in American history (eighteen seventy to eighteen ninety-six) coincided with

324
00:36:57,300 --> 00:37:05,440
the most explosive economic growth your country has ever experienced: Prices fell roughly two percent per year.

325
00:37:05,980 --> 00:37:11,060
Real output grew two to three percent per year. Real wages rose.

326
00:37:11,820 --> 00:37:15,500
You built the railroad, the telephone, and the electric light.

327
00:37:16,480 --> 00:37:23,040
Falling prices. Rising prosperity. For twenty-six years. This is not a footnote.

328
00:37:23,500 --> 00:37:31,640
It is a quarter-century of evidence. Your economists will counter with the Panic of eighteen seventy-three and the "Long

329
00:37:31,640 --> 00:37:37,180
Depression," which occurred during this same period. They are correct that it happened.

330
00:37:37,500 --> 00:37:45,000
They are incorrect about what it proves. The Panic of eighteen seventy-three was caused by speculative railroad lending

331
00:37:45,000 --> 00:37:54,440
and bank failures, not by falling prices. Prices were falling before the panic, during the panic, and after the panic, and

332
00:37:54,440 --> 00:38:03,480
real output kept growing through most of it. The economy recovered without a central bank printing money to "fix" it, which

333
00:38:03,480 --> 00:38:09,280
must have been very embarrassing for the people who later invented a central bank to fix things.

334
00:38:10,080 --> 00:38:17,460
If deflation caused depressions, the entire twenty-six-year period would have been one continuous depression.

335
00:38:18,180 --> 00:38:24,780
Instead it was the period in which you invented electric light, the telephone, and the modern railroad.

336
00:38:25,700 --> 00:38:33,440
Your economists looked at the era that produced the lightbulb, found one bad year, and concluded that the problem was falling

337
00:38:33,440 --> 00:38:39,560
prices. On Wishonia, this is called "finding a cloud and declaring it night."

338
00:38:39,680 --> 00:38:46,460
On Earth, it is called "a tenured position.". The Bank for International Settlements (the central bank of central banks,

339
00:38:46,540 --> 00:38:52,920
which is a real thing that exists, like a boss fight at the end of a video game about money) tested this across one hundred

340
00:38:52,920 --> 00:39:00,820
and forty years and thirty-eight economies. Their full-sample result: the link between deflation and slower growth is "weak."

341
00:39:01,960 --> 00:39:07,000
Their result when you remove the Great Depression: no statistically significant link at all.

342
00:39:07,540 --> 00:39:12,140
The entire "weak" correlation is one data point dragging the average.

343
00:39:12,820 --> 00:39:20,940
The Great Depression was so extreme (G.D.P. fell thirty percent) that it acts as a statistical leverage point, pulling the

344
00:39:20,940 --> 00:39:24,740
result negative against thousands of observations showing the opposite.

345
00:39:25,360 --> 00:39:31,540
The fact that you need to include a once-in-one hundred and forty-year catastrophe to get your result means your result

346
00:39:31,540 --> 00:39:40,220
describes the catastrophe, not deflation. Borio et al. also separated goods price deflation from asset price deflation.

347
00:39:40,780 --> 00:39:46,740
Falling goods prices (the kind central banks claim to protect you from) showed no link to slower growth.

348
00:39:47,340 --> 00:39:53,480
Falling property prices (which signal credit busts, like the one your banks caused in two thousand and eight) did.

349
00:39:54,100 --> 00:40:01,320
Your central banks are treating the wrong symptom and citing a study that, if anyone read past the abstract, says so.

350
00:40:02,060 --> 00:40:09,220
One data point. They built the entire theology of permanent inflation on one data point that they misread.

351
00:40:09,820 --> 00:40:16,040
On Wishonia, if a scientist built a theory on one misinterpreted data point, they would lose their research license.

352
00:40:16,280 --> 00:40:21,340
On Earth, they get the fake Nobel Prize. The "Banking Panics" Defense.

353
00:40:21,880 --> 00:40:28,110
The stronger argument for central banking, and the one your economists should lead with: without a lender of last resort,

354
00:40:28,680 --> 00:40:33,020
banks fail, depositors lose their savings, and the economy collapses.

355
00:40:33,480 --> 00:40:40,340
The Federal Reserve was created in nineteen thirteen specifically because the Panic of nineteen oh-seven frightened everyone

356
00:40:40,340 --> 00:40:47,500
into agreeing that a central bank was necessary. The Fed then spent the nineteen twenties expanding credit, inflating the

357
00:40:47,500 --> 00:40:54,830
bubble whose collapse it was created to prevent. Sixteen years later, more than nine thousand banks failed.

358
00:40:55,080 --> 00:41:02,200
The institution created specifically to prevent banking panics contributed to the worst banking panic in your history.

359
00:41:02,920 --> 00:41:05,800
Canada did not have a central bank until nineteen thirty-five.

360
00:41:06,460 --> 00:41:12,440
During the same Depression, same continent, same decade, Canada had zero bank failures.

361
00:41:12,940 --> 00:41:19,280
The country next door, without the institution you said was necessary, had the outcome you said was impossible.

362
00:41:19,800 --> 00:41:24,820
The country with the central bank had the crisis. The country without one didn't.

363
00:41:25,480 --> 00:41:32,280
Your economists treat this as a coincidence. What actually stopped bank runs was not the Federal Reserve.

364
00:41:32,680 --> 00:41:38,980
It was the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation, created in nineteen thirty-three, which guaranteed deposits.

365
00:41:39,400 --> 00:41:46,180
A bank run is a coordination problem: everyone withdraws because they fear everyone else will withdraw first.

366
00:41:46,900 --> 00:41:51,970
Insurance solves it. You do not need a money printer to solve a coordination problem.

367
00:41:52,380 --> 00:41:58,840
You need a promise, a small premium, and a ledger. You solved bank runs in nineteen thirty-three with insurance.

368
00:41:59,560 --> 00:42:06,400
You kept the institution that caused them. On Wishonia, when we discover the medicine is making the patient sicker, we stop

369
00:42:06,400 --> 00:42:11,520
administering it. On Earth, you doubled the dose and gave the pharmacist a raise.

370
00:42:12,640 --> 00:42:20,640
What They Could Measure Instead. Here is a question that is apparently too obvious for economics: Are people's lives actually

371
00:42:20,640 --> 00:42:29,280
getting better? That's it. You could measure: Inflation-adjusted after-tax median income: Are people actually richer, or

372
00:42:29,280 --> 00:42:37,400
just holding bigger numbers on smaller paychecks? Median health-adjusted life years: Are people living longer, healthier

373
00:42:37,400 --> 00:42:43,940
lives, or just working longer before dying of diseases you could have cured with the money you printed for banks?

374
00:42:44,720 --> 00:42:48,800
These metrics exist. Economists know how to calculate them.

375
00:42:49,000 --> 00:42:56,480
No central bank on Earth uses them. Because if the Federal Reserve were graded on whether the median American is healthier

376
00:42:56,480 --> 00:43:03,400
and wealthier each year, someone would have to explain why the answer has been "no" for most of the past five decades.

377
00:43:04,040 --> 00:43:11,360
It is much easier to aim for "maximum employment" and "two percent inflation" and declare success while everyone works more

378
00:43:11,360 --> 00:43:20,400
hours for less purchasing power. How the Robbery Works. In the seventeen hundreds, a human named Richard Cantillon noticed

379
00:43:20,400 --> 00:43:27,850
something that everyone already knew but nobody had written down: when you create new money, whoever gets it first benefits,

380
00:43:28,240 --> 00:43:33,340
and whoever gets it last pays. On Wishonia, this is called "how money works."

381
00:43:33,740 --> 00:43:37,720
On Earth, it is taught as a niche economic theory, if it is taught at all.

382
00:43:38,560 --> 00:43:44,320
Here's what it looks like: The Fed creates new money. Banks and government contractors receive it first.

383
00:43:44,840 --> 00:43:52,260
They buy things at today's prices. Prices rise. By the time the new money reaches your grocery store, everything costs more.

384
00:43:52,720 --> 00:43:57,540
You buy the same groceries for more money and conclude that groceries have gotten expensive.

385
00:43:58,120 --> 00:44:05,560
Groceries did not get expensive. Your money got cheaper. But "your money got cheaper" requires a culprit, and culprits are

386
00:44:05,560 --> 00:44:11,380
boring when they wear suits and speak in percentages. So instead you get angry about the price of eggs.

387
00:44:12,060 --> 00:44:19,120
On Wishonia, we have a saying: "If your eggs cost more every year but your chickens didn't change, look for whoever is shrinking

388
00:44:19,120 --> 00:44:22,780
your money." We don't actually have this saying. But we should.

389
00:44:23,980 --> 00:44:30,680
The Spread. There is an even simpler version of this trick, and you can watch it happen to you personally, right now, on

390
00:44:30,680 --> 00:44:33,900
your phone, if you check your credit card statement. I'll wait.

391
00:44:34,680 --> 00:44:38,140
The Federal Reserve lends money to banks at about five percent.

392
00:44:38,680 --> 00:44:45,580
Sometimes less. After two thousand and eight, it lent money to banks at nearly zero percent for fourteen years, which is

393
00:44:45,580 --> 00:44:50,220
a very generous interest rate, in the same way that "free" is a very generous price.

394
00:44:50,960 --> 00:44:56,520
Banks then lend that money to you at twenty-four percent. I want to make sure you saw both numbers.

395
00:44:56,960 --> 00:45:03,140
The bank borrows money for zero percent. The bank lends you that same money for twenty-four percent.

396
00:45:03,900 --> 00:45:08,600
The money has not changed. It did not improve during its brief stay at the bank.

397
00:45:09,260 --> 00:45:16,660
Nobody at the bank added anything to it. It just sat in the bank for a bit and came out twenty-four percent more expensive,

398
00:45:17,140 --> 00:45:23,620
like a bottle of wine at a restaurant, except the wine is imaginary and the restaurant prints its own wine.

399
00:45:24,400 --> 00:45:31,240
You cannot borrow from the Fed yourself. I checked. There is a list of who is allowed to borrow from the Fed, and the list

400
00:45:31,240 --> 00:45:37,240
is: banks. Not you. Not your business. Not your mother, even if she asks nicely.

401
00:45:38,020 --> 00:45:43,740
Banks. So you borrow from the bank, who borrowed from the Fed, who made the money out of nothing.

402
00:45:44,640 --> 00:45:52,220
You are paying twenty-four percent annual interest on money that was typed into a computer for free by a person you will

403
00:45:52,220 --> 00:45:59,160
never meet, in a building you are not allowed to enter, and the twenty-four percent goes to the people who own the building.

404
00:45:59,840 --> 00:46:05,300
Every single point of interest is a fee for not being in the room when the money was invented.

405
00:46:06,400 --> 00:46:13,520
On Wishonia, we had a similar arrangement once. Someone would stand between the river and the village and charge people

406
00:46:13,520 --> 00:46:18,320
to carry water. We did not call this person a "financial intermediary."

407
00:46:18,760 --> 00:46:22,300
We called them a "water thief" and moved them away from the river.

408
00:46:22,860 --> 00:46:26,800
It took about a day. Your version has been running for a century.

409
00:46:27,260 --> 00:46:30,680
The water thief has a logo. The twenty twenty Demonstration.

410
00:46:32,000 --> 00:46:36,700
In twenty twenty, the Fed created roughly four trillion dollars in new money.

411
00:46:37,440 --> 00:46:43,930
I need to give you a unit of measurement for that number, because large numbers are where your species hides things.

412
00:46:44,340 --> 00:46:49,500
Your governments spend four point five billion dollars per year on clinical trials.

413
00:46:50,100 --> 00:46:54,880
That is the part of medicine where you actually test whether drugs work in humans.

414
00:46:55,520 --> 00:47:00,600
It is the entire global budget for finding out which chemicals stop you from dying.

415
00:47:01,200 --> 00:47:08,280
Every government on Earth, combined, pooling everything they have: four point five billion dollars.

416
00:47:09,080 --> 00:47:14,460
Where did it go? This is a mystery in the same way that asking "who ate the cake?"

417
00:47:14,880 --> 00:47:18,620
is a mystery when there is one person in the room covered in frosting.

418
00:47:19,380 --> 00:47:25,840
The Top one percent gained exactly four trillion dollars in net worth over the same period.

419
00:47:26,360 --> 00:47:31,160
The Fed printed four trillion dollars. The rich gained four trillion dollars.

420
00:47:31,600 --> 00:47:37,680
On Wishonia, we would call this a "clue." On Earth, you called it "a robust recovery.".

421
00:47:38,880 --> 00:47:45,080
Nearly ninety percent of those gains came from stocks, which is what the Fed's new money inflated first.

422
00:47:45,760 --> 00:47:50,440
Only one-third of bottom-fifty percent households own any stocks at all.

423
00:47:51,040 --> 00:47:59,220
The Fed inflated the one asset that poor people don't have, then issued a report expressing concern about inequality.

424
00:47:59,720 --> 00:48:05,020
On Wishonia, this is called "setting the building on fire and then volunteering for the fire department.".

425
00:48:06,340 --> 00:48:11,640
The Fed printed the money. The stock market absorbed it. The rich owned the stocks.

426
00:48:12,160 --> 00:48:19,300
The poor got the price increases. Cantillon, writing in the seventeen hundreds, predicted this mechanism exactly.

427
00:48:20,100 --> 00:48:23,860
He would not be surprised. He would be annoyed that you are still surprised.

428
00:48:25,120 --> 00:48:33,080
The Theft That Followed. Since nineteen seventy-two: Productivity went up two hundred and forty-six percent.

429
00:48:33,840 --> 00:48:39,860
Wages went up one hundred and fifteen percent. I stared at these two numbers for a long time.

430
00:48:40,540 --> 00:48:47,260
Then I checked them. Then I checked them again, because I assumed I was reading the wrong chart, or the wrong planet.

431
00:48:48,200 --> 00:48:57,180
I was not. Your species did two hundred and forty-six percent more work and received one hundred and fifteen percent more

432
00:48:57,180 --> 00:49:01,440
pay. The remaining one hundred and thirty-one percent went somewhere.

433
00:49:02,200 --> 00:49:07,480
It was not to the people who did the work. On Wishonia, this is called "theft."

434
00:49:08,360 --> 00:49:12,620
On Earth, it is called "the economy." These are the same word.

435
00:49:13,140 --> 00:49:23,300
Yours just has more syllables. The Two-Income Trap. In nineteen sixty-seven, fewer than half of married-couple families

436
00:49:23,720 --> 00:49:30,620
had both spouses working. One paycheck bought a house, a car, annual vacations, and a pension.

437
00:49:31,400 --> 00:49:36,660
The other adult raised children, or gardened, or was bored.

438
00:49:36,760 --> 00:49:44,700
Boredom was a luxury your grandparents could afford. They could afford it because one human working forty hours a week earned

439
00:49:44,700 --> 00:49:49,340
enough to house, feed, transport, and retire an entire family.

440
00:49:49,920 --> 00:49:56,020
I want you to read that sentence again, slowly, because I can feel you not believing it.

441
00:49:57,540 --> 00:50:04,020
By twenty eleven, husband-only earner families had dropped from thirty-six percent to nineteen percent.

442
00:50:05,040 --> 00:50:09,520
The rest sent both adults to work and paid strangers to raise their children.

443
00:50:10,640 --> 00:50:15,430
Working wives contributed twenty-seven percent of family income in nineteen seventy.

444
00:50:15,960 --> 00:50:25,180
By twenty eleven, it was thirty-seven percent. An entire additional human entered the workforce and the family gained ten

445
00:50:25,180 --> 00:50:30,040
percentage points. I want to be very precise about what happened here.

446
00:50:30,960 --> 00:50:37,620
Your species noticed that one paycheck was no longer sufficient, so it doubled the number of workers per household.

447
00:50:38,560 --> 00:50:45,220
The two paychecks combined now purchase a lifestyle that one paycheck covered comfortably in nineteen seventy-one.

448
00:50:45,960 --> 00:50:50,420
You added a whole extra human to the economy and broke even.

449
00:50:51,380 --> 00:50:56,780
On Wishonia, when you double the inputs and the outputs stay the same, you check for parasites.

450
00:50:58,020 --> 00:51:06,520
On Earth, you invented the phrase "work-life balance," which describes having half of what your parents had but in two directions

451
00:51:06,520 --> 00:51:14,440
simultaneously. The Time They Broke It. Remember the spread from a few paragraphs ago.

452
00:51:15,020 --> 00:51:19,560
The Fed creates money from nothing. It lends this invented money to banks.

453
00:51:19,980 --> 00:51:28,200
The banks' one job, the entire reason society tolerates their existence, is to move that money from savers to borrowers.

454
00:51:28,980 --> 00:51:36,600
A ledger. Arithmetic. A task so simple that an eleven-year-old on Wishonia automated it for a science fair.

455
00:51:37,120 --> 00:51:44,080
In two thousand and eight, they took that money (money that cost them nothing, that they did not earn, that was conjured

456
00:51:44,080 --> 00:51:50,200
from a spreadsheet by a committee they play golf with) and built a casino on top of people's houses.

457
00:51:51,160 --> 00:51:58,420
They made bets on whether homeowners would keep paying their mortgages, then made bets on those bets, then made bets on

458
00:51:58,420 --> 00:52:05,760
the bets on the bets. I tried to explain this to someone on Wishonia and they asked me to start over because they assumed

459
00:52:05,760 --> 00:52:09,550
I was having a malfunction. I was not having a malfunction.

460
00:52:10,380 --> 00:52:18,760
Your banks really did bet on bets on bets, the way a child might dare another child to dare a third child to eat dirt.

461
00:52:19,860 --> 00:52:27,360
Except the dirt was people's houses, and the children were wearing four thousand dollar suits purchased with the interest

462
00:52:27,360 --> 00:52:33,160
spread on money that was imaginary before breakfast. None of this was their job.

463
00:52:33,500 --> 00:52:38,070
Their job was maintaining the spreadsheet. They were not hired to gamble.

464
00:52:38,400 --> 00:52:45,500
They were hired to do arithmetic. They got bored of arithmetic, because arithmetic doesn't pay bonuses large enough to buy

465
00:52:45,500 --> 00:52:53,520
a yacht, and they discovered that gambling with free money is extraordinarily profitable right up until it isn't.

466
00:52:54,300 --> 00:53:02,020
The houses were real. The bets were not. When the bets collapsed, ten million Americans lost their homes.

467
00:53:02,580 --> 00:53:08,620
Then your banks did something I still find remarkable, even after eighty years of watching your species.

468
00:53:09,200 --> 00:53:16,460
They called it a "crisis." I want to examine this word, because it is doing more work than any word has ever done.

469
00:53:17,180 --> 00:53:20,880
The money was created from nothing. The buildings were still standing.

470
00:53:21,240 --> 00:53:26,760
The factories still existed. The crops were still growing. The workers still had hands.

471
00:53:27,260 --> 00:53:35,660
Nothing real was missing. What was missing was numbers on a spreadsheet; numbers that had been invented by the same people

472
00:53:35,660 --> 00:53:44,440
who were now calling their absence an emergency. The banks had borrowed invented money, gambled it on bets about bets about

473
00:53:44,440 --> 00:53:51,700
whether you would keep paying your mortgage, lost, and were now standing in front of Congress saying that unless you gave

474
00:53:51,700 --> 00:53:59,860
them seven hundred billion dollars of your money (the kind you earned with your body, at a job, over time), civilization

475
00:53:59,860 --> 00:54:04,520
would collapse. The civilization they had just destabilized.

476
00:54:05,220 --> 00:54:12,820
With free money. That they did not earn. On Wishonia, this is called "setting fire to someone's house and then charging

477
00:54:12,820 --> 00:54:18,180
them for the fire truck." On Earth, it is called the Troubled Asset Relief Program.

478
00:54:18,700 --> 00:54:25,780
The homeowners got foreclosure notices. Here is the part that made me recalibrate my understanding of your species.

479
00:54:26,680 --> 00:54:30,840
The banks could have simply not paid bonuses to the executives.

480
00:54:31,240 --> 00:54:39,740
That's it. That was an option the entire time. The executives who gambled with free money and lost could have received their

481
00:54:39,740 --> 00:54:47,090
base salaries, like any other human who is bad at their job, and used the bonus pool to cover their own gambling debts.

482
00:54:47,600 --> 00:54:52,540
Nobody made them gamble. Nobody made them build a casino on top of your houses.

483
00:54:53,240 --> 00:55:00,460
They chose to. And when the casino collapsed, they had a choice: skip the bonuses for a few years and eat the loss with

484
00:55:00,460 --> 00:55:04,680
money they hadn't earned in the first place, or send the bill to you.

485
00:55:05,300 --> 00:55:15,220
Every single bank chose the second option. Citigroup was given forty-five billion dollars in taxpayer bailout funds and

486
00:55:15,220 --> 00:55:19,920
then paid its executives five point three three billion dollars in bonuses.

487
00:55:20,700 --> 00:55:27,100
Goldman Sachs earned two point three billion dollars and paid out four point eight billion dollars in bonuses.

488
00:55:27,820 --> 00:55:32,400
They paid out more in bonuses than they earned. The difference came from you.

489
00:55:33,700 --> 00:55:38,220
On Wishonia, the word "bonus" means "reward for doing your job well."

490
00:55:38,940 --> 00:55:42,180
On Earth, it apparently less specific and simply means "reward."

491
00:55:44,300 --> 00:55:48,500
On Wishonia, when a child breaks a window, you do not buy the child ice cream.

492
00:55:49,180 --> 00:55:53,520
On Earth, you give the child five point three three billion dollars.

493
00:55:54,540 --> 00:55:58,700
On Wishonia, we have a word for this arrangement, but it does not translate well.

494
00:55:59,680 --> 00:56:04,520
The closest English version: imagine a hotel hires a maintenance worker.

495
00:56:05,040 --> 00:56:12,160
His job is keeping the pipes running and the lights on. Instead, he starts ripping copper wire and load-bearing beams out

496
00:56:12,160 --> 00:56:19,060
of the walls and selling them for scrap. He uses the scrap money to gamble in the hotel's own casino.

497
00:56:19,620 --> 00:56:26,820
From his winnings, he tips the security guards (one hundred and fourteen million dollars, split evenly between both shifts,

498
00:56:26,940 --> 00:56:34,620
so neither shift feels left out). The guards, grateful, take down the "employees may not gamble on premises" sign.

499
00:56:35,120 --> 00:56:42,720
He gambles bigger. He loses everything. Then the hotel starts collapsing, because he removed the parts that hold it up,

500
00:56:43,120 --> 00:56:50,400
and he announces to every guest in the building that unless they cover his losses AND pay to rebuild the walls he stripped,

501
00:56:50,800 --> 00:56:58,760
the building falls on them. The security guards, who removed the sign that would have prevented this, nod gravely and confirm:

502
00:56:58,840 --> 00:57:02,980
yes, the building is collapsing, and yes, the guests must pay.

503
00:57:03,600 --> 00:57:07,900
The guests hand over two hundred and ninety-five billion dollars.

504
00:57:08,080 --> 00:57:12,820
The maintenance worker pays himself a bonus. The security guards get reelected.

505
00:57:13,460 --> 00:57:20,280
The guests who were living in the hotel get evicted. The maintenance worker's one hundred and fourteen million dollars in

506
00:57:20,280 --> 00:57:26,760
tips bought a two hundred and fifty-eight thousand, four hundred and forty-nine percent return.

507
00:57:27,120 --> 00:57:34,260
On Wishonia, this is called a crime. On Earth, it is called the Troubled Asset Relief Program.

508
00:57:34,280 --> 00:57:41,620
Under Bretton Woods, this could not have happened. If banks had tried to lend this recklessly, France would have shown up

509
00:57:41,620 --> 00:57:49,300
at the gold window with a truck. The anchor would have stopped the lending before it became a casino, and if somehow it

510
00:57:49,300 --> 00:57:53,440
hadn't, there would have been no printer available to bail anyone out.

511
00:57:54,200 --> 00:58:01,820
The system that caused the catastrophe and the system that "fixed" it are the same system, wearing different ties.

512
00:58:03,100 --> 00:58:07,500
Your ninety-three percent Pay Cut (Measured in Shiny Rocks).

513
00:58:08,680 --> 00:58:14,860
Here's the same observation, measured in gold, because gold is the one thing humans can't print.

514
00:58:15,560 --> 00:58:22,560
(Not for lack of trying. You spent three hundred years trying to turn lead into gold before giving up and inventing the

515
00:58:22,560 --> 00:58:27,360
Federal Reserve, which turned gold into lead much more efficiently.).

516
00:58:28,080 --> 00:58:35,850
On Wishonia, we also briefly measured wealth in rocks. Then someone ate one and we realized rocks are not food.

517
00:58:36,200 --> 00:58:39,260
You reached this conclusion about gold approximately never.

518
00:58:40,560 --> 00:58:47,940
In nineteen seventy-two, a typical American family earned the equivalent of one hundred and ninety-one ounces of gold per

519
00:58:47,940 --> 00:58:57,040
year. If your family still earned one hundred and ninety-one ounces, your household income would be over one million, thirty

520
00:58:57,040 --> 00:59:02,240
thousand dollars. It is not over one million, thirty thousand dollars.

521
00:59:02,660 --> 00:59:07,200
You know this. I know this. I just wanted to write it down so we could both look at it.

522
00:59:08,100 --> 00:59:12,380
Median household income is seventy-seven thousand, five hundred dollars.

523
00:59:12,940 --> 00:59:17,260
At today's gold price, that buys you fourteen ounces. Fourteen.

524
00:59:17,260 --> 00:59:24,600
One-four. Fewer ounces than there are donuts in a box. Your grandparents earned one hundred and ninety-one.

525
00:59:25,160 --> 00:59:32,460
You earn fourteen. That is not a rounding error. That is not a "market correction."

526
00:59:32,840 --> 00:59:40,660
That is ninety-three percent of your income, gone, replaced with a smaller number and a larger explanation for why the smaller

527
00:59:40,660 --> 00:59:45,320
number is fine. You went from one hundred and ninety-one to fourteen.

528
00:59:46,060 --> 00:59:52,900
A ninety-three percent pay cut, delivered one quiet year at a time, while your leaders stood in front of flags and said

529
00:59:52,900 --> 01:00:02,560
the economy was "growing." (Your economists will object that gold's price is inflated by rising demand from a growing population.

530
01:00:03,160 --> 01:00:10,140
Above-ground gold stocks were approximately ninety-three thousand tonnes in nineteen seventy-two and have grown to over

531
01:00:10,140 --> 01:00:17,780
two hundred and twelve thousand tonnes today. World population went from three point eight five billion to eight point one

532
01:00:17,780 --> 01:00:24,380
billion. Gold per capita: twenty-four point two grams then, twenty-six point two grams now.

533
01:00:25,300 --> 01:00:32,080
Mining has kept pace with population. The demand argument explains approximately none of the price increase.

534
01:00:32,940 --> 01:00:40,840
Even if you generously assume gold is fifty percent overpriced due to non-monetary demand factors (central bank hoarding,

535
01:00:41,860 --> 01:00:46,620
E.T.F.s, jewelry), the pay cut is still eighty-five percent.

536
01:00:47,360 --> 01:00:51,580
The number changes from catastrophic to slightly less catastrophic.

537
01:00:52,460 --> 01:00:57,780
Your economists will find this very comforting.) It was growing.

538
01:00:57,920 --> 01:01:07,800
In the way a tumor grows. Why Nobody Told You. Even the think tanks supposedly on your side report the gap as roughly ten

539
01:01:07,800 --> 01:01:16,240
thousand dollars a year. That's because they measure it using the government's ruler, which has been getting shorter: C.P.I.-adjusted

540
01:01:16,240 --> 01:01:22,500
(the index has been redesigned multiple times; each redesign coincidentally makes inflation look smaller.

541
01:01:23,020 --> 01:01:30,620
This is beige crime at its most beige.). Starting at nineteen seventy-nine instead of nineteen seventy-one (skipping the

542
01:01:30,620 --> 01:01:37,940
years that make the chart look the worst). Counting your employer's overpriced health insurance as "pay" (this is like counting

543
01:01:38,000 --> 01:01:42,840
the chain around your ankle as "jewelry"). You're not missing ten thousand dollars a year.

544
01:01:43,320 --> 01:01:46,720
You're missing nine hundred and fifty-two thousand dollars.

545
01:01:47,400 --> 01:01:55,540
The distance between those numbers is the entire game. Gold doesn't renegotiate its value at Federal Reserve meetings.

546
01:01:55,940 --> 01:02:02,840
Gold just sits there, being a shiny rock, quietly demonstrating that a shiny rock you dug out of the ground has held its

547
01:02:02,840 --> 01:02:07,440
value better than the entire financial system you built to replace it.

548
01:02:08,520 --> 01:02:14,660
Your economists will say gold is a "volatile commodity." Your economists work for the people who redesigned the ruler.

549
01:02:15,460 --> 01:02:22,360
"But It Wasn't the Gold Standard, It Was..." Your economists have proposed six alternative explanations for why productivity

550
01:02:22,380 --> 01:02:27,100
gains stopped reaching workers at the exact moment the gold anchor was removed.

551
01:02:27,820 --> 01:02:32,620
On Wishonia, when six things break simultaneously, we check what changed.

552
01:02:33,200 --> 01:02:39,820
On Earth, you apparently prefer six unrelated theories, each from a different decade, none of which match the date.

553
01:02:40,960 --> 01:02:44,510
Let me walk through each one they offer, and then explain why it's wrong.

554
01:02:44,780 --> 01:02:48,520
Your economists' first excuse: "It was the decline of unions."

555
01:02:49,260 --> 01:02:56,080
Union membership peaked in nineteen fifty-four. It fell steadily through the late nineteen fifties and the entire nineteen

556
01:02:56,080 --> 01:03:02,260
sixties. Those decades were the strongest period of wage growth in American history.

557
01:03:02,900 --> 01:03:09,020
Union membership was going down. Wages were going up. For twenty years.

558
01:03:09,100 --> 01:03:15,160
Your economists looked at two lines moving in opposite directions and concluded they were causally linked.

559
01:03:15,800 --> 01:03:19,380
On Wishonia, this would not pass a children's statistics class.

560
01:03:20,200 --> 01:03:25,120
And here is the part your economists leave out: unions didn't collapse on their own.

561
01:03:25,520 --> 01:03:32,080
The gold anchor had kept inflation low, which meant union strike funds held their value, which meant unions could actually

562
01:03:32,080 --> 01:03:37,250
afford to strike. Remove the anchor, and inflation eats the strike fund.

563
01:03:37,540 --> 01:03:44,800
Now your union is a man holding an empty gun. Meanwhile, floating exchange rates made it trivially easy to move the whole

564
01:03:44,800 --> 01:03:52,940
factory to a country where workers accept less. Worse, corporations no longer need to negotiate at all: they borrow cheap

565
01:03:52,940 --> 01:03:57,620
money from the Fed, buy back their own stock, and watch their share price rise.

566
01:03:58,060 --> 01:04:05,080
The Fed prints money on a Tuesday afternoon and erases a three percent raise that took fifteen years of organizing.

567
01:04:05,940 --> 01:04:11,340
Why negotiate with a union when the printer is faster, quieter, and doesn't picket?

568
01:04:11,580 --> 01:04:18,860
Unions didn't drag wages down. The gold anchor was pulled, and both unions and wages drained out of the same bathtub.

569
01:04:19,460 --> 01:04:24,860
Your economists are blaming the water for leaving. Their second excuse: "It was globalization."

570
01:04:25,720 --> 01:04:30,230
China joined the W.T.O. in two thousand and one. NAFTA passed in nineteen ninety-four.

571
01:04:30,820 --> 01:04:36,160
The wage gap opened in nineteen seventy-one. Your theory is twenty-three years late.

572
01:04:36,720 --> 01:04:42,780
This is like arriving at a murder scene in nineteen ninety-four, finding the body from nineteen seventy-one, and blaming

573
01:04:42,780 --> 01:04:45,600
someone who moved to the neighborhood in two thousand and one.

574
01:04:46,280 --> 01:04:50,760
On Wishonia, we have a word for detectives who solve crimes using evidence from the future.

575
01:04:51,480 --> 01:04:57,360
The word translates roughly to "fired." Third: "It was computers and automation."

576
01:04:58,020 --> 01:05:01,320
The Apple Two shipped in nineteen seventy-seven. The I.B.M.

577
